# Unity does not read the .csproj beside this file. It reads the .asmdef and this response file,
# so <Nullable>enable</Nullable> in the project reaches dotnet and CI and reaches Unity not at all.
# Without this the annotated source compiles under a disabled nullable context and every `string?`
# in the assembly raises CS8632 — ~230 across the seven core packages, none of them a defect, all
# of them buried in the Editor console where a real warning has to be found.
#
# One line per assembly rather than a project-wide switch: Unity's response files are matched by
# assembly NAME in the folder holding the .asmdef, and there is no inherited form.
#
# RETIREMENT TRIGGER: if this package stops being consumed by Unity (its .asmdef is deleted, or
# the core moves off netstandard2.1 and Unity loads a prebuilt DLL instead), delete this file with
# the .asmdef — an orphaned .rsp is silently ignored and reads as a setting that is in force.
-nullable:enable
